    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe exception class and the `ExceptionControllerAdvice` handler are clean \u2014 they follow the established `service`-package-exception + matching `handleXxx` pattern exactly (the new handler is a faithful twin of `handleInvalidPostcodeException`, right down to the 400 and log shape). No issue there.\n\nThe one structural inconsistency is in `ClinicServiceImpl.saveOwner`:\n\n- This class has a clear, repeated idiom for input validation: extract each check into a private `assert*` method and call it up front. `assertPostcodeValid(owner)` is the direct precedent \u2014 it is exactly this same category (a 400-mapped, input-validation guard that throws a service exception) and it lives in its own method, invoked as the first line of `saveOwner`. `assertDailyCapacity`, `assertCityCapacity`, and `assertIdentityUnique` follow the same convention.\n- The new future-date validation instead is inlined into the middle of the normalization/defaulting block, sitting between address normalization and the `effectiveDate` defaulting. This breaks the symmetry: a maintainer scanning `saveOwner` would expect all validation to appear as `assert*` calls near the top, not one guard inlined among the mutations.\n\nSuggested change: extract the check into a private method matching the existing idiom (e.g. `assertRegistrationDateNotFuture(owner)`, with a short Javadoc like the other assert methods and `@throws FutureRegistrationDateException`) and call it alongside `assertPostcodeValid(owner)` at the top of `saveOwner`. Compute `LocalDate.now()` locally inside that method as the other self-contained asserts do; `saveOwner` can keep its own `today`/`effectiveDate` defaulting. That keeps `saveOwner` a coherent validate \u2192 normalize \u2192 persist sequence and keeps the future-date rule with its peers.",
